{"qid": "c02", "family": "c", "split": "dev", "method": "M3", "model": "glm", "answer": {"method": "M3", "model": "glm", "final": {"answer": "The item directly upstream of CHV-2-2 is FV-2-2, which is controlled by the FIC-2-2 loop. According to the Fisher datasheet (fisher-et-d100022x012), the Fisher ET is a single-port, globe-style control valve with cage guiding, a balanced valve plug, and push-down-to-close valve plug action. So yes, it is the valve body of a globe-style (sliding-stem) control valve, part of the Fisher easy-e family, used for throttling or on-off control of liquids and gases.", "not_present": false}, "outcome": "ANSWERED", "route": ["context_rag+chunks"], "cypher": [], "sources": [{"document_id": "fisher-et-d100022x012", "pages": [29, 36, 1, 8, 9, 16, 31, 3, 2, 22, 4]}, {"document_id": "flowserve-worcester-f51-f52", "pages": [11]}], "latency_s": 11.238116199965589, "usage": {"prompt_tokens": 14713, "completion_tokens": 1991, "cost_usd": 0.0003234705, "spent_usd": 0.0003230905, "n_calls": 2, "n_cached": 1, "models": ["qwen/qwen3-embedding-8b", "z-ai/glm-5.3-flash"]}, "trace": {"chunks": [{"document_id": "fisher-et-d100022x012", "page": 29, "score": 0.6535}, {"document_id": "fisher-et-d100022x012", "page": 36, "score": 0.6516}, {"document_id": "fisher-et-d100022x012", "page": 1, "score": 0.6384}, {"document_id": "fisher-et-d100022x012", "page": 8, "score": 0.6342}, {"document_id": "fisher-et-d100022x012", "page": 9, "score": 0.6054}, {"document_id": "fisher-et-d100022x012", "page": 16, "score": 0.6015}, {"document_id": "fisher-et-d100022x012", "page": 31, "score": 0.599}, {"document_id": "fisher-et-d100022x012", "page": 3, "score": 0.5886}, {"document_id": "fisher-et-d100022x012", "page": 2, "score": 0.5866}, {"document_id": "fisher-et-d100022x012", "page": 22, "score": 0.5773}, {"document_id": "flowserve-worcester-f51-f52", "page": 11, "score": 0.574}, {"document_id": "fisher-et-d100022x012", "page": 4, "score": 0.5728}], "prompt_chars": 39025, "reply": "{\"answer\": \"The item directly upstream of CHV-2-2 is FV-2-2, which is controlled by the FIC-2-2 loop. According to the Fisher datasheet (fisher-et-d100022x012), the Fisher ET is a single-port, globe-style control valve with cage guiding, a balanced valve plug, and push-down-to-close valve plug action. So yes, it is the valve body of a globe-style (sliding-stem) control valve, part of the Fisher easy-e family, used for throttling or on-off control of liquids and gases.\", \"not_present\": false}"}}}
{"qid": "c03", "family": "c", "split": "dev", "method": "M3", "model": "glm", "answer": {"method": "M3", "model": "glm", "final": {"answer": "The transmitter feeding FIC-2-2 is FT-2-2, whose datasheet is the Rosemount 3051 (rosemount-3051-00813-0100-4001). Per that datasheet (p. 88), when self-diagnostics detect a sensor or microprocessor failure, the analog signal is driven either high or low to alert the user; the direction is user-selectable with a jumper/switch on the transmitter. The default is NAMUR compliant: high alarm ≥ 21.75 mA and low alarm ≤ 3.75 mA. The alarm direction/levels can also be configured via option codes: C4 (NAMUR alarm high), CN (NAMUR alarm low), CR/CS (custom high/low alarm levels, requiring option code C1), and CT (Rosemount standard low alarm); these alarm-level options are only available with the HART 4-20 mA output (code A).", "not_present": false}, "outcome": "ANSWERED", "route": ["context_rag+chunks"], "cypher": [], "sources": [{"document_id": "rosemount-3051-00813-0100-4001", "pages": [88, 15, 109, 46, 35, 86, 83, 57, 62, 74, 26, 42]}], "latency_s": 11.939375000016298, "usage": {"prompt_tokens": 12779, "completion_tokens": 1786, "cost_usd": 0.0002829426, "spent_usd": 0.0002825826, "n_calls": 2, "n_cached": 1, "models": ["qwen/qwen3-embedding-8b", "z-ai/glm-5.3-flash"]}, "trace": {"chunks": [{"document_id": "rosemount-3051-00813-0100-4001", "page": 88, "score": 0.5841}, {"document_id": "rosemount-3051-00813-0100-4001", "page": 15, "score": 0.5226}, {"document_id": "rosemount-3051-00813-0100-4001", "page": 109, "score": 0.5218}, {"document_id": "rosemount-3051-00813-0100-4001", "page": 46, "score": 0.5168}, {"document_id": "rosemount-3051-00813-0100-4001", "page": 35, "score": 0.5164}, {"document_id": "rosemount-3051-00813-0100-4001", "page": 86, "score": 0.5066}, {"document_id": "rosemount-3051-00813-0100-4001", "page": 83, "score": 0.5065}, {"document_id": "rosemount-3051-00813-0100-4001", "page": 57, "score": 0.4993}, {"document_id": "rosemount-3051-00813-0100-4001", "page": 62, "score": 0.4971}, {"document_id": "rosemount-3051-00813-0100-4001", "page": 74, "score": 0.4939}, {"document_id": "rosemount-3051-00813-0100-4001", "page": 26, "score": 0.49}, {"document_id": "rosemount-3051-00813-0100-4001", "page": 42, "score": 0.4869}], "prompt_chars": 37266, "reply": "{\"answer\": \"The transmitter feeding FIC-2-2 is FT-2-2, whose datasheet is the Rosemount 3051 (rosemount-3051-00813-0100-4001). Per that datasheet (p. 88), when self-diagnostics detect a sensor or microprocessor failure, the analog signal is driven either high or low to alert the user; the direction is user-selectable with a jumper/switch on the transmitter. The default is NAMUR compliant: high alarm ≥ 21.75 mA and low alarm ≤ 3.75 mA. The alarm direction/levels can also be configured via option codes: C4 (NAMUR alarm high), CN (NAMUR alarm low), CR/CS (custom high/low alarm levels, requiring option code C1), and CT (Rosemount standard low alarm); these alarm-level options are only available with the HART 4-20 mA output (code A).\", \"not_present\": false}"}}}
